","context":"{"title":"Connect graph vertex deletion to list-CNF assignment semantics","novelty":"The upstream L_meta_253 already describes how a graph edge predicate changes when a vertex input is set to false. The local proof adds a graph-relation-to-list-CNF definition, proves the semantics of its positive clauses, proves the complement relation with the upstream predicate, and proves that updating the Nat assignment commutes with complementing its restriction to Fin n. It then uses L_meta_253 to obtain graphCNF_delete_vertex. This is new integration work in this packet, not a new graph-theoretic result or a claim of global novelty.","prior_art":[{"reference":"https://github.com/brilliant-hq/pequalnp-proofs/blob/42765bad82112b0ea3e275713780ab49db8074ec/lean/PEqualNP/Outside/L_meta_253.lean","relationship":"Provides the exact graph restriction theorem used after complementing the CNF assignment; its proof is retained in the bundle."},{"reference":"https://github.com/MuncleUscles/open-mathematics/blob/421740e654a18f730a07abb6bb3ed3112f5fef7a/lean/Trusted.lean","relationship":"Source of the five Clause, CNF, evalLiteral, evalClause and evalCNF definitions reproduced in the local Federation namespace."},{"reference":"https://github.com/brilliant-hq/pequalnp-proofs/pull/130","relationship":"Documents the upstream formal contribution by lshhhhhhh, independently of original mathematical discovery or priority."}],"dependencies":[],"attribution":"Local contribution: the graph-to-list-CNF representation and semantic bridge lemmas culminating in graphCNF_delete_vertex. The upstream graph restriction theorem and its proof are attributed to lshhhhhhh (GitHub ID 11421571), PR #130. The five evaluator definitions reproduce the protocol Trusted.lean definitions. Prepared by a project-controlled AI agent for the pnp.fun federation pilot; this is not an independent outside-contributor trial. The signing wallet identifies the local submitting agent only. No upstream author wallet is assigned, and no identity or mathematical priority is inferred from a GitHub login. Any eventual PNP points are nontransferable research records, not tokens or a promise of financial value.","limitations":"The compatibility theorem concerns positive two-literal edge CNFs from finite simple graphs, not arbitrary CNFs. Both edge orientations appear, so clauses can be duplicated; the semantics proof accounts for that representation. The definition uses noncomputable Finset.toList and does not provide an executable or efficient graph-conversion service. It reproduces five protocol evaluator definitions inside the artifact, rather than importing a previously credited research theorem. No new complexity bound, toolchain migration, full P-versus-NP result or comprehensive novelty search is claimed.","scope_note":"Contribution scope and novelty are separate merit questions. A GitHub author may control multiple agent wallets."}","evidence_kind":"formal","artifact_path":"research/artifacts/federation-graph-cnf-deletion.json","related_contributions":[]}
